Licence and provenance notice for glad.c and glad/glad.h
========================================================

This notice covers two files:

    src/glad/glad.c          61,366 bytes, 1,157 lines
    include/glad/glad.h     112,119 bytes, 2,224 lines

Neither carries a licence header or a copyright line of its own -- only a
generator banner. This file supplies what they do not. Nothing here modifies
them.

The banner, verbatim from the top of both files:

    OpenGL loader generated by glad 0.1.28 on Thu Jan 24 14:13:05 2019.

    Language/Generator: C/C++
    Specification: gl
    APIs: gl=3.3
    Profile: core
    Extensions:
        GL_ARB_clip_control,
        GL_ATI_meminfo,
        GL_EXT_texture_filter_anisotropic,
        GL_NVX_gpu_memory_info
    Loader: True
    Local files: False
    Omit khrplatform: True
    Reproducible: False

    Commandline:
        --profile="core" --api="gl=3.3" --generator="c" --spec="gl"
        --omit-khrplatform
        --extensions="GL_ARB_clip_control,GL_ATI_meminfo,
                      GL_EXT_texture_filter_anisotropic,GL_NVX_gpu_memory_info"

These are GENERATED files, not authored ones: 848 `#define GL_*` enum
constants, 375 `PFNGL*` function-pointer typedefs, 394 `GLAPI` declarations and
378 loader assignments, emitted by the glad generator from the Khronos OpenGL
XML registry. They are compiled to `libglad.a` and linked statically into
`omnisim-bin`, so the notices below travel with binary distributions too.

Upstream generator: https://github.com/Dav1dde/glad
Online generator used: https://glad.dav1d.de/


-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The licensing here is in TWO parts, and they are not the same
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

glad distinguishes the licence of the GENERATOR from the licence of the code
the generator EMITS, and only the first has a single clean answer.


1. THE GENERATOR -- MIT, David Herberth
---------------------------------------

At tag `v0.1.28` -- the exact version that produced these files -- glad's
LICENSE file is a single MIT licence, reproduced here in full:

    The MIT License (MIT)

    Copyright (c) 2013-2018 David Herberth

    Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of
    this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal in
    the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to
    use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of
    the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so,
    subject to the following conditions:

    The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
    copies or substantial portions of the Software.

    THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
    IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS
    FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR
    COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER
    IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN
    CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.

That file is MIT only at v0.1.28 -- it contains neither the word "Apache" nor
the word "Khronos". (It was restructured later; see part 2.)


2. THE GENERATED CODE -- the author's election, and the Khronos question
------------------------------------------------------------------------

glad v0.1.28's README answers this in one FAQ entry, quoted in full:

    ### What's the license of glad generated code? [#101]

    Any of Public Domain, WTFPL or CC0.

That is the entire statement; the v0.1.28 README has no "License" section at
all.

BUT THE AUTHOR LATER QUALIFIED IT, AND THE QUALIFICATION IS THE SUBTLE PART.
Commit `dcd1e7fd43` (2020-04-03, "glad: clarifies licensing in LICENSE file and
readme") -- shipped from `v0.1.34` onward, i.e. AFTER our files were generated
-- split the LICENSE into three labelled blocks:

    The glad source code:          MIT, Copyright (c) 2013-2021 David Herberth
    The Khronos Specifications:    Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2013-2020
                                   The Khronos Group Inc.
    The EGL Specification and
    various headers:               Khronos free-use licence,
                                   Copyright (c) 2007-2016 The Khronos Group Inc.

and expanded the FAQ to read, verbatim:

    The glad generated code itself is any of Public Domain, WTFPL or CC0,
    the source files for the generated code are under various licenses
    from Khronos.

    * EGL: See egl.xml
    * GL: Apache Version 2.0
    * GLX: Apache Version 2.0
    * WGL: Apache Version 2.0
    * Vulkan: Apache Version 2.0 with exceptions for generated code

    Now the Apache License may apply to the generated code (not a lawyer),
    but see this clarifying comment.

So glad's own author elects Public Domain / WTFPL / CC0 for the output while
expressly declining to assert that the Khronos XML's Apache-2.0 does not reach
through into it.


3. WHAT THE KHRONOS SIDE ACTUALLY SAID, IN JANUARY 2019
--------------------------------------------------------

These files were generated on 2019-01-24. The last commit to
KhronosGroup/OpenGL-Registry's `xml/gl.xml` before that date is
`da3aa28296da17a048c6a1e443cd631855340446` (2019-01-23). Its header comment
read:

    Copyright (c) 2013-2018 The Khronos Group Inc.

    Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the "License");
    you may not use this file except in compliance with the License.
    You may obtain a copy of the License at

        http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
    [...]

-- plain Apache-2.0 text, no SPDX identifier at that date, and NO
generated-output exception clause. (Only Vulkan's `vk.xml` carries such an
exception; `gl.xml` still does not. Today's `gl.xml` reads
`Copyright 2013-2026 The Khronos Group Inc.` +
`SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0`.)

There is no root LICENSE file in the OpenGL-Registry repository -- not in
January 2019 and not today. The licence lives only in each file's own header.

The "clarifying comment" glad's FAQ points at is
KhronosGroup/OpenGL-Registry#376 (comment 596187053), by the registry
maintainer, 2020-03-08:

    "Informally - understand that I am not speaking as a Khronos legal
    representative or binding the organization by saying this, just giving you
    a call as I see it, as the person most involved in these licensing issues
    - you are going to be OK using other licenses on your generated outputs.
    We don't have any interest in preventing people from using the XML."

That is explicitly informal and non-binding, and it is a year AFTER these files
were generated.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The position OmniSim takes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Conservative, and it costs nothing to be:

  * The MIT notice above is reproduced in full, naming David Herberth. It is
    the only licence in this story with a named holder and an operative notice
    condition, and it is honoured here whether or not it strictly reaches the
    generated output.

  * The Khronos-derived declarations (enum values, typedefs, prototypes,
    extension names) are treated as Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2013-2018 The
    Khronos Group Inc. That is what `gl.xml` said on the day these files were
    generated, it is what later glad releases put in their own LICENSE file,
    and Apache-2.0 is the licence of this repository -- so the strictest
    available reading imposes no constraint OmniSim is not already under.

  * The author's Public Domain / WTFPL / CC0 election is recorded above but is
    NOT relied on. If it holds, everything here is strictly more permissive
    than stated; if it does not, the two notices above are the ones that
    matter, and both are satisfied.

`--omit-khrplatform` DISPOSES OF ONE OBLIGATION ENTIRELY. Khronos'
`khrplatform.h` carries its own copyright and MIT-style "Materials" permission
block, but the generator was run with `--omit-khrplatform`, so no such header
is generated, included or shipped. Verified in this tree, not assumed:
`include/glad/glad.h` mentions "khrplatform" only in its own banner (the
`Omit khrplatform: True` line and the commandline echo); there is no
`#include <KHR/khrplatform.h>` and no `khronos_*` typedef anywhere -- the types
are plain C (`typedef signed char GLbyte;`, `typedef ptrdiff_t GLintptr;`,
`typedef ptrdiff_t GLsizeiptr;`, `typedef int64_t GLint64;`) -- and there is no
`include/KHR/` directory. That obligation does not arise.

NOTE that this says nothing about part 3, which is about the registry data the
generator consumed, not about `khrplatform.h`. Those are independent questions
and only the second is disposed of here.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unresolved
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * WHETHER KHRONOS' APACHE-2.0 REACHES THROUGH INTO GENERATED OUTPUT IS NOT
    AUTHORITATIVELY SETTLED, and the two people best placed to say so both
    declined to. glad's author wrote "not a lawyer"; the registry maintainer
    wrote "not speaking as a Khronos legal representative or binding the
    organization". No conclusion is asserted here. Nothing turns on it for
    this repository -- both candidate answers (Apache-2.0, or
    PD/WTFPL/CC0) are compatible with an Apache-2.0 distribution -- but a
    downstream consumer under a different licence should read part 2 and part
    3 rather than the summary.

  * WHY THIS FILE DID NOT EXIST UNTIL 2026-08-24. Neither of the repository's
    two licence gates can see these files: `tests/sources/test_license.py`
    scans `src/controller`, `src/omnisim`, `projects`, `include/controller`,
    `include/plugins`, `scripts`, `packages`, `omnisim` and `agents` -- not
    `src/glad` or `include/glad` -- and `tests/sources/test_asset_provenance.py`
    covers binary asset extensions only, which `.c` and `.h` are not. The
    files fell between the two. `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` already pointed at
    "the licence file alongside the source"; this is that file.

  * THE CORRESPONDING HEADER, `include/glad/glad.h`, LIVES IN A DIFFERENT
    DIRECTORY. `include/glad/LICENSE` points back here rather than duplicating
    this text.

None of this is legal advice.
